\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ:Israel's Fischer Wins Kudos For Central Bk Role Amid Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Mitnick   \par \uc2 Of The Wall Street Journal   \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2009\par 14:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV (Dow Jones)--Bank of Israel Gov. Stanley Fischer stepped into the global spotlight late last month when he raised interest rates, the first major \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief to do so amid the global economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, economists have been applauding the former International Monetary Fund and \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . (C) executive for months, crediting him with adroitly steering the Jewish state's export-driven economy into what now looks like an early rebound.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal Web site, WSJ.com).\par \par \uc2 After slowing to a near-standstill this year, Israel's economy is forecast to grow 3.3% next year, according to \uc2 HSBC Holdings PLC\uc2  (HBC) economists. In a recent report, the bank said Israel has shown "tremendous resilience to the global recession" and cited Fischer's active monetary policy over the last year for that performance.\par \par \uc2 In mid-2008, Israel appeared as vulnerable as any when the extent of the global downturn first started to become clear. The country's banking system and real-estate sectors appeared healthy. But exports make up about 45% of the country's economy. The prospect of sharp downturns in big consuming countries like the U.S. and Europe had Israeli industrialists bracing for a steep fall in overseas orders.\par \par \uc2 Fischer, like other central bankers around the world, slashed interest rates, cutting from 4.25% in October 2008 to 0.5% early this year. Crucially, Fischer also suspended \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and started \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 From May 2008 through the end of last month, Fischer spent $28.4 billion, or about 14% of Israel's gross domestic product, buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  never said it was acting to weaken the Israeli shekel, the purchases did just that, helping to keep Israeli exports competitively priced.\par \par \uc2 When the full force of the global downturn hit this year, Israeli exports fell. But they didn't fall as much as many had feared. Exporters praised Fischer's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Manufacturers' Association of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects exports to drop an inflation-adjusted 13% this year. But without Fischer's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the damage would have been worse, says Ori Yehudai, chairman of the trade group's economic committee.\par \par \uc2 "It could have been a disaster," he says, adding that Fischer "was very proactive and showed leadership." With the global economic outlook looking better more recently, the bank has slowed down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fischer came to \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top banking post in 2005. His decision to immigrate to Israel from the U.S. - where he was working as a senior executive at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  - won lots of early fans here.\par \par \uc2 He arrived well prepared for crisis. As the IMF's deputy managing director in the late 1990s, he was at the forefront of efforts to stem the back-to-back Asian and Russian financial crises.\par \par \uc2 His stint at the Bank of Israel hasn't been without critics. Some economists blame the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for stoking Israeli inflation. It was rising prices that prompted Fischer to raise interest rates by 25 basis points last month, from 0.5% to 0.75%.\par \par \uc2 Bank officials defend their actions, and say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't stoke excessive inflation. Barry Topf, the head of the markets division at the Bank of Israel, said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials realized that for a small, open economy, heavily dependent on exports, the combination of a global economic slump with an "unjustifiably" strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been too painful not to act. [ 09-09-09 0857ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020090909e5990007r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
