\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Israel c.bank to hold rates but hike by end-year\b0\par\par\uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2009\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * What: Israeli interest rates\par \par \uc2 * When: Monday, Aug 24, 1430 GMT\par \par \uc2 * 9 of 12 economists see no rate move; 2 see quarter point hike, 1 sees half point rise\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The Bank of Israel is expected to leave short-term borrowing costs unchanged for a fifth straight month next week, but high inflation and a surprise return to economic growth has economists anticipating a rate hike by the end of 2009.\par \par \uc2 In what is expected to be a difficult call given a desire to boost economic growth while containing growing inflation pressures, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will decide on rates on Monday. Nine of 12 economists polled by Reuters forecast no move from the current interest rate of 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 But three economists expect Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer will raise the interest rate -- two saying by a quarter percentage point and one by a half-point -- following data in recent days indicating a faster economic recovery than expected.\par \par \uc2 Like many banks, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  had forecast the start of rate hikes in the fourth quarter at the earliest but it now expects a half-point increase already next week, citing a track record of earlier than expected changes in rates.\par \par \uc2 "The risk of a deflationary depression is declining, making the case for a very easy monetary policy stance less and less compelling," said emerging markets economist Miroslav Plojhar.\par \par \uc2 Published data on Sunday showed Israel's economy grew by an annualised 1 percent in the second quarter versus forecasts for a third straight quarterly decline. The data signalled a likely end to a recession in Israel. [ID:nLG207492]\par \par \uc2 That followed a report on Friday showing the consumer price index rose a higher than expected 1.1 percent in July from June and 3.5 percent over the prior 12 months. The GDP contraction in the first quarter was revised to 3.2 percent, less severe than earlier estimates. [ID:nLAG003602]\par \par \uc2 Most economists played down both reports, saying they were filled with one-time factors such as government tax increases boosting inflation and a drop in imports helping growth.\par \par \uc2 And while that may delay rate hikes, only four of the 12 economists expected Fischer to wait until 2010. Rates are expected to rise to 2 to 4.5 percent over the next year.\par \par \uc2 TIMING UNCLEAR\par \par \uc2 Those not forecasting a hike next week are split as to the timing of a policy tightening, with some saying September, October, November or December.\par \par \uc2 "He (Fischer) will be more prudent and wait for a better view of the economy," said Alon Katz, head of research at the Maor-Luski Investment House in Tel Aviv, echoing the thoughts of other analysts who believe that raising rates now would stifle the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Katz said October was most likely since it will come after the September inflation report, which will not have the one-time factors from taxes seen in July and August.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel forecasts a contraction of 1.5 percent for 2009. But economists now see zero growth this year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lowered its key rate by 3.75 points in the period from last October to March. It supplemented its easing policy with buying government bonds and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In announcing no rate move in July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also halted its bond purchase programme. In early August, it ended a more than one-year programme of buying $100 million a day in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a means to weaken the shekel and boost exports.\par \par \uc2 "Both were preconditions to expect an approaching start of an interest rate hiking cycle, in our view," said Plojhar.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on days of "unsual movements".\par \par \uc2 "With unconventional policy measures removed, the timing of rate hikes has come into focus," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  economist Caroline Grady wrote in a client note. "We see Israel as one of the first countries to move back to a hiking cycle."\par \par \uc2 But Grady believes that won't be until 2010 since unemployment is still rising and there is "considerable slack" that should keep price pressures subdued even as the economy improves in the second half of 2010.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Stephen Nisbet)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-RATES/ (POLL)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090818e58i000o6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
