\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Israel central bank \highlight\uc2  expands  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2009\par 15:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep buying $100 of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  a day\par \par \uc2 * Will act in event of "unusual movements"\par \par \uc2 * Shekel slides, then recovers\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say central banks can't beat the markets\par \par \uc2 (Adds analysts' comments)\par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, Aug 3 (Reuters) - Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday said it would widen its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the shekel <ILS=> continues to appreciate against the dollar and threatens to hurt an already battered export sector.\par \par \uc2 The move comes a week after the Bank of Israel became the first \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end a radical monetary easing step of buying government bonds. [ID:nLR150230]\par \par \uc2 For more than a year, the Bank of Israel has been buying $100 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a day, partly to try and stem the shekel's rise. It said it will continue to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "From now on, the Bank of Israel will act in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the event of unusual movements in the exchange rate which are inconsistent with underlying economic conditions, or when conditions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are disorderly," it said in a brief statement.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the statement was too vague since "unusual movements" are subjective and they predicted the move would ultimately backfire, since the Bank of Israel was now adding a new challenge to speculators.\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Israel is trying to send a message they will do whatever it can to prevent an appreciation of the shekel," said Gil Bufman, chief economist at Bank Leumi in Tel Aviv.\par \par \uc2 "But what we have seen in the past ... the market is always stronger than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 The shekel weakened sharply on the news to 3.83 per dollar from its official rate of 3.743, set about an hour before the announcement. The official rate was the strongest level since Dec. 19, 2008.\par \par \uc2 In late trading, shekel moved back to 3.79 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Once the dust settles, the shekel will recover," said Shlomo Maoz, chief economist at the Nessuah Excellence brokerage.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which gained 1.2 percent on Monday, had appreciated by about 12 percent since reaching a low of 4.25 per dollar in late April.\par \par \uc2 SPECULATORS, EXPORTS\par \par \uc2 Much of the advance came from speculators. Bufman said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement plays into the hands of speculators who will likely use the "artificial" weakness of the shekel to bet against it, continue to sell dollars and then buy them back at cheaper levels.\par \par \uc2 "The only way you are going to make life more difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators is to increase \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  uncertainty," Bufman said.\par \par \uc2 As the shekel has continued to rise, exporters have appealed to politicians and Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer to act to stem its advance since they have been battered by a low global demand, the weak dollar and rising Israeli inflation.\par \par \uc2 Exports, which account for nearly half of Israel's economic activity, slumped an annualised 29 percent in the first quarter under pressure from the global economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 The economy contracted by an annualised 3.7 percent the first three months of the year. The Bank of Israel predicts a moderation in the second half of 2009.\par \par \uc2 Economists believe the shekel has no choice but to appreciate further due to a host of factors in the shekel's favour -- Israel's economy suffering less than the United States, large foreign capital inflows and a current account surplus in Israel's balance of payments.\par \par \uc2 "That creates a pretty strong set of fundamentals pointing in the direction of an appreciation of the shekel," Bufman said.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, a number of foreign banks such as \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , BoA \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  have advised clients to buy shekels over dollars.\par \par \uc2 Maoz believes the best way to help exporters would be to raise short-term interest rates, which currently are 0.5 percent. He argued that since rates are so low, money has poured into the stock exchange, which has surged in recent months.\par \par \uc2 "Once you raise rates, the stock exchange won't go up like crazy and people will go into other assets like the dollar," he said.\par \par \uc2 Bufman believes the money being spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be better spent on providing aid to exporters since the current policy is just fueling inflation pressures. He said helping exporters should be solved by fiscal policies.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Richard Balmforth)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /CENBANK (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020090803e583001a8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
