\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel worried by shekel surge, officials eye options\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph Nasr   \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2009\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV, June 2 (Reuters) - Israeli government officials on Tuesday expressed concern that gains for the shekel <ILS=> would harm the economy, and Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's top economic adviser warned of possible government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is doing a great job to keep the shekel weaker against the dollar," Ori Yogev, who advises Netanyahu as the chair of the National Economic Council's advisory committee, told a conference.\par \par \uc2 "If efforts (to weaken the shekel) do not work, the government of Israel would have to find the tools to preserve a real exchange rate," said\par \par \uc2 After weakening by some 25 percent since last July to 4.26 per dollar by late April, the shekel -- mostly due to global losses for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- has rebounded about 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 It gained another 1.8 percent on Monday to a fresh four-month high, before retreating 0.6 percent on Tuesday to 3.911 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yogev, speaking at a business conference in Tel Aviv, did not say at what rate the government would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but added: "It would be an economic catastrophe if the dollar goes back to 3.5 (shekels) or below."\par \par \uc2 To stem the shekel's run last year, the Bank of Israel in March 2008 started to buy small amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day, raising it to $100 million a day last July when the shekel reached 3.20 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day and has not indicated when it would stop.\par \par \uc2 Exports, which account for nearly half of the country's economic activity, have been hit hard due to a very strong shekel for the first half of 2008 and then a deepening global economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Israeli exports are in trouble because of lower demand and the stronger shekel," Finance Minister Yuval Steinitz told the same conference.\par \par \uc2 He made no comment on the chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said the state would provide loan guarantees of up to 26 billion shekels to help minimise the damage.\par \par \uc2 RECOVERY THIS YEAR?\par \par \uc2 Exports slid more than an annualised 46 percent in the first three months of 2009, leading to a 3.6 percent contraction of Israel's economy on an annual basis in the first quarter.\par \par \uc2 The economy is forecast to contract by as much as 1.5 percent in 2009 and then grow a modest 1 percent in 2010.\par \par \uc2 "We still face months and months of negative growth but we are edging towards the end of the crisis," Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer told the conference.\par \par \uc2 He cited swift measures taken by many Western countries to counter the crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Had it not been for the global efforts from leading countries to stop the crisis we would not be sitting here talking about the possibility of recovery by the end of this year or early next year," Fischer said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its key short-term borrowing rate by 3.75 percentage points between last October and late March to a record low of 0.5 percent. It has not altered rates at its last two policy meetings, shifting its focus to buying government bonds to try and lower long-term rates.\par \par \uc2 (Writing by Steven Scheer; editing by Patrick Graham)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-ECONOMY/ (UP