\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Israel shekel up 1.4 pct vs dlr to 11-1/2-year high\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2008\par 14:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, May 27 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel <ILS=> advanced 1.4 percent to an 11-1/2 year high against the dollar on Tuesday after the Bank of Israel raised short-term interest rates for the first time in five months.\par \par \uc2 The shekel's official rate was set at 3.2850 per dollar -- its strongest level since January 21, 1997 -- from Friday's rate of 3.3330. Rates were not set on Monday due to holidays in Britain and the United States.\par \par \uc2 Late on Monday, the Bank of Israel raised its key lending rate by a quarter-point to 3.5 percent as expected in a bid to combat rising inflation pressures. It was the first rate increase since last December and another rate hike is expected next month.\par \par \uc2 The market began pricing in a rate hike in mid-May after consumer prices jumped a more than expected 1.5 percent in April on the heels of gains in food and energy costs to bring annual inflation to 4.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 "Yesterday's hike by the Bank of Israel is unlikely to be one-off given that we see inflation yet peaking at above 5 percent year-over-year in May," Silja Sepping, an emerging markets analyst at Lehman Brothers, wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 The rate hike came after two half-point rate cuts in February and March on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's view that a U.S. downturn would hurt Israel's economy and moderate inflation.\par \par \uc2 But soaring food and energy prices have fueled inflation, while Israel's economy grew at an annualised rate of 5.4 percent in the first quarter -- well above expectations.\par \par \uc2 "We think another 25 basis point hike in June seems likely," Sepping said. "Given the Bank of Israel's tightening bias and its seemingly higher tolerance of shekel strength, we stay short dollar-shekel in our model portfolio."\par \par \uc2 The shekel has appreciated by 17 percent against the shekel so far in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel is opposed to such a strong shekel on the grounds that it makes Israeli exports less competitive and will ultimately dent the economy. In March, when the shekel reached 3.35 per dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the dollar's slide.\par \par \uc2 So far, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the current dollar slide, which has been more orderly than in March.\par \par \uc2 "Higher inflation, together with dollar-shekel having moved through previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, suggests that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now more willing to see the shekel appreciate," wrote JP Morgan emerging markets strategist Nandita Singh.\par \par \uc2 A stronger shekel tends to offset some inflation pressures since about 40 percent of home rents are quoted in dollars but paid in shekels.\par \par \uc2 Tel Aviv share indexes were up 1.1 percent in late trading while bond prices <ILSAHR2683=TA> rose 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The shekel rose 1.25 percent against the euro to 5.7152.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Gerrard Raven)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ISRAEL/SHEKEL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080527e45r000vp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
