\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - UPDATE 1-Shekel jumps vs dollar on Israel-Syria peace talks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2008\par 18:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects fourth paragraph to say Turkey mediating Israel-Syria peace talks)\par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, May 21 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel <ILS=> jumped more than 1 percent in late trading on Wednesday to a more than 11-year peak against the dollar, triggered by news that Israel and Syria had resumed indirect peace talks.\par \par \uc2 At 1450 GMT the shekel was at 3.33 per dollar, its strongest level since February 1997 and above its official rate at 3.3620. It had hit an intraday high of 3.32.\par \par \uc2 Key share indexes on the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange rose about 1 percent and benchmark 10-year bond prices ended 0.4 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 The shekel opened around 3.38 per dollar and appreciated to 3.36 by mid-afternoon after Israel and Syria announced they had launched indirect peace talks mediated by Turkey, the first confirmation of negotiations between the long-time enemies in eight years.\par \par \uc2 The exchange rate held between 3.35 and 3.36, a key support level that was the previous high for the shekel versus the dollar in March. It was at that point, 3.35, when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, buying $600 million of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to halt a slide in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "There was the announcement today that Israel and Syria were starting a peace process so people started selling dollars," said Moran Yellink, a dealer at \uc2 Israel Discount Bank.\par \par \uc2 "Once it got to 3.35, it (the dollar) fell like a rock. Everybody sold -- locals, banks and even foreigners."\par \par \uc2 The shekel has appreciated more than 15 percent so far this year against the dollar after a 10 percent gain in 2007.\par \par \uc2 Such a move puts Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer in a tough spot. A strong shekel helps to contain inflationary pressures since nearly half of housing costs in Israel are quoted in dollars but paid in shekels.\par \par \uc2 But a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also tends to hurt exports, which would threaten to weigh on the economy as a whole.\par \par \uc2 The shekel has moved back to its mid-March highs after a surprising 1.5 percent jump in the consumer price index in April from March, bringing inflation over the prior 12 months to a rate of 4.7 percent -- well above an official target of 1 to 3 percent.\par \par \uc2 The market had expected a monthly rise of 0.9 percent and an annual rate of 4.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 As a result, market players have factored in at least a quarter-point rate increase next week as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to rein in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel had lowered its key rate by a combined one percentage point in February and March to a record low of 3.25 on expectations that a U.S. slowdown would dampen Israeli growth and ease inflation pressures.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ron Askew)\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-SHEKEL/ (UPDATE 1) (CORRECTED)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080521e45l0017j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
