\par\par \b \uc2 Israel shekel gains to 6-week high vs dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2008\par 15:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, May 6 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel <ILS=> advanced another 0.6 percent against the dollar on Tuesday to a six-week high amid growing risk appetite that has boosted emerging markets across the board.\par \par \uc2 The shekel's official rate was set at 3.4160 per dollar -- its strongest level since March 20 -- compared with 3.4370 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 After the setting of the official rate, dollar-shekel stood at 3.42.\par \par \uc2 So far in 2008, the shekel has appreciated more than 13 percent against the dollar and is close to its 11-year peak of 3.35 set on March 20 -- a level that prompted the Bank of Israel to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in a decade in a bid to prevent a further drop in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "This recovery leaves the Bank of Israel in a difficult position, as it follows signs of inflation pressure and a less dovish tone from the Federal Reserve, which make rate cuts tougher to justify," RBS analyst Lucy Bethell wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 "It suggests that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, it will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again," she added.\par \par \uc2 A strong shekel hurts \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy by making exports less attractive.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowererd its key lending rate by one point earlier this year but last week held rates at 3.25 percent on the heels of persistent inflation pressures.\par \par \uc2 The Fed lowered U.S. interest rates by a modest quarter percentage point to 2 percent last Wednesday and hinted that the move could be the last in the series that has seen rates fall by 3.25 percentage points since September.\par \par \uc2 The move widened the gap between Israeli and U.S. rates to 1.25 points.\par \par \uc2 A positive differential for Israel has helped to underpin the shekel so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel will set official rates on Wednesday at 0930 GMT -- three hours early -- due to the Memorial Day holiday. There will be no trading on Thursday due to Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 Tel Aviv share indexes <.TA25> <.TA100> were down 1 percent in late trading, while benchmark bond prices <ILSAHR2683=TA> slipped 0.2 percent to yield 5.78 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Tel Aviv bourse is closed on Wednesday and Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the shekel <EURILS=> was set at 5.3001 compared with 5.3138 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 On the crosses, the shekel was set at 3.2578 per 100 yen compared with 3.2662 on Monday and against the pound at 6.7304 compared with 6.7693.\par \par \uc2 For further information double click on the following:\par \par \uc2 <ILS=> for latest commercial bank shekel/dollar rates.\par \par \uc2 <ILS1=> for a list of commercial bank shekel/dollar rates.\par \par \uc2 <ILS2=> for a list of commercial bank shekel/basket rates.\par \par \uc2 <BOIT12> for Bank of Israel rates.\par \par \uc2 <FXIL> for Israeli spot and mid rates by the hour.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Steven Scheer)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ISRAEL/SHEKEL|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|MD|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080506e4560013u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
