\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Bank of Israel cuts key rate by 50 bps to 3.25 pct   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 17:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement, shekel reaction)   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, March 24 (Reuters) - Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut its key lending rate by half a percentage point to a record low of 3.25 percent, it said on Monday, citing expectations that a severe global slowdown will curb Israeli growth and moderate inflation pressures.   \par \par \uc2 "The forces that served to moderate price increases last month -- mainly the higher probability of a slowdown in the global economy and the strengthening of the shekel -- continued to do so this month even more strongly," the Bank of Israel said in a statement.   \par \par \uc2 A similar move a month ago came as a surprise to the markets and was not fully supported by other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials.   \par \par \uc2 However, Monday's announcement was widely expected and follows an announcement last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would boost foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by about $10 billion over two years starting on March 24 through the purchase of $25 million of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  every day.   \par \par \uc2 "The global interest rate environment has fallen and is expected to continue to fall further," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 "Thus a reduction in the Bank of Israel interest rate is required this month too due to the moderating effect on price rises in Israel of the interest rate differentials between Israel and abroad, via the strengthening of the shekel."   \par \par \uc2 The Israeli move, which takes effect on Thursday, follows the latest U.S. rate cut last week in a series totalling three percentage points since September that has reduced the U.S. benchmark to 2.25 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on March 13 for the first time in more than a decade as the globally weak dollar threatened to hurt Israeli exports and overall economic growth.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Israel's moves have helped boost the dollar from an 11-year low of below 3.38 shekels <ILS=> last Wednesday to above 3.52 on Monday -- a 4 percent jump.   \par \par \uc2 The shekel strengthened by 10 percent versus the dollar in 2007 and until last week, it was up another 14 percent so far in 2008.   \par \par \uc2 "The degree of the strengthening of the shekel against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-December 2007 in itself acts to lower the pressures for price increases in the next few months," the Bank of Israel said, adding the link between the dollar-shekel rate and inflation was weakening.   \par \par \uc2 Inflation remains high in Israel, with consumer prices up 3.6 percent over the prior 12 months in February to exceed an official target range of 1 to 3 percent a year.   \par \par \uc2 But with the economy expected to slow in 2008 to below a 3.5 percent rate from 5.3 percent in 2007, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes inflation will be closer to 2 percent by the end of the year.   \par \par \uc2 "The half-point rate reduction is likely to support continued economic growth and employment," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. "The expected slowdown in Israel's growth reflects the decline in exports and in private spending expected to result from the slowdown in the global economy."   \par \par \uc2 (Editing by Ruth Pitchford)   \par \p