\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Markets-Spreads near widest since June 05, shekel down   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn   \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2008\par 12:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 14 (Reuters) - Emerging sovereign debt spreads approached their widest levels since June 2005 on Friday as global risk aversion continued to bite, and the shekel retreated from near-11-year highs after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 However local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond markets clawed back some recent losses triggered by a global liquidity squeeze.   \par \par \uc2 Sovereign bond spreads widened 6 basis points to 303 bps over U.S. Treasuries <11EMJ> as the dollar set new record lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and investors showed reluctance to hold higher-yielding assets.   \par \par \uc2 Spreads last closed above 306 bps in June 2005. Emerging debt spreads hit record tight levels below 150 bps in June 2007.   \par \par \uc2 "The basic issue under debate is the growth story and the impact of the slowing U.S. economy on different markets," said Oliver Stoenner, head of emerging asset allocation research at Cominvest in Frankfurt.   \par \par \uc2 "Latest data from India for instance gives the impression that growth is slowing and on the other side you have inflation problems, so emerging central banks have no real scope to give any stimulus to the economy. It's not a favourable mix for markets."   \par \par \uc2 India's annual growth is expected to slow in the first quarter as the economy eases towards a trend rate of 8.0-8.5 percent, the country's chief statistician said this week.   \par \par \uc2 Benchmark emerging equities dipped 0.3 percent <.MSCIEF> on Friday towards recent four-week lows.   \par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the shekel slid versus the dollar <ILS=> after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt its run to near 11-year highs. Dealers said the bank bought about $200 million in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, and $50 million on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 The shekel had risen over 3 percent to a high of 3.3465 per dollar on Thursday ahead of the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , accelerating gains after  \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said it might upgrade \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's A2 ratings.   \par \par \uc2 The shekel was trading at 3.47 at 1050 GMT, down more than 1 percent from the U.S. close.   \par \par \uc2 Hungary's forint hit a 2-1/2 week high versus the euro <EURHUF=>, as 5-year yields eased off last week's 3-1/2 year highs.   \par \par \uc2 Peripheral European bond markets had suffered ahead of the Fed's $200 billion liquidity injection this week, as investors favoured more liquid markets.   \par \par \uc2 Hungary's debt agency plans no further measures or changes to 2008 issuance plan and expects the local market turbulence to be temporary, the agency's chief executive said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Turkish bond yields also dropped, reversing recent sharp rises, and the lira <TRY=> steadied.   \par \par \uc2 "We see Hungary coming back a lot and performing well after the sell-off last week," said a Germany-based trader. "There is the same situation in Turkey, with yields some 10-15 bps lower."   \par \par \uc2 Ukraine's hyrvnia <UAH=> also firmed against the broadly weak dollar, heading towards the 5.0 level which marks the upper boundary of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's normal 5.0-5.06 trading corridor.   \par \par \uc2 The Icelandic crown steadied at 109.20 per euro <EURISK=> after a 6 percent sell-off against the euro in the past two days, to a record low beyond 111.50.   \par \par \uc2 Century Aluminum\uc2  said on Friday it was starting site preparation for a 250,000 tonne aluminium smelter in Iceland, after winning approval for the project on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 "This could be a story that would at least halt the rapid weakening in the last two days, especially if we see more developments globally and on emerging markets," said Jon Bentsson, economist at Glitnir in Reykjavik. (Additional reporting by Sujata Rao, editing by David Christian-Edwards)   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EMERGING|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|MD|HX|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080317e43e001k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
