\par\par \b \uc2 Israel shekel up vs dollar but most early gains erased   \b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2007\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel <ILS=> edged up to hold near a 5-1/2-month peak against the dollar on Thursday after the Federal Reserve cut U.S. rates, but erased nearly all of its early gains on the view the Fed may not ease again.   \par \par \uc2 The shekel's official rate was set at 3.9640 per dollar from Wednesday's rate of 3.9660 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Fed's quarter-point rate reduction to 4.50 percent -- just 50 basis points above Israel's key lending rate -- the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at 3.9478 but the dollar gradually gained throughout the day, hitting a high of 3.9730 shekels.   \par \par \uc2 The move mirrored that of the euro which hit a record high of above $1.45 before retreating to $1.4420.   \par \par \uc2 "The shekel is very influenced by what happens with the euro/dollar (rate)," said a trader at a major bank.   \par \par \uc2 The shekel has gained more than 9 percent against the dollar since late July, and many dealers believe it is on track to revisit an 8-1/2-year high of 3.93/dollar reached in mid-May.   \par \par \uc2 In late trading after the official rate was set the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 3.9775 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 At a conference in Tel Aviv on Thursday, Bank of Israel Governor Stanley Fischer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not give up its tradition of non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial markets.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday left Israel's key lending rate unchanged at 4 percent, noting that the robust shekel contained inflation to well within an official annual target range of 1 to 3 percent.   \par \par \uc2 The Fed's rate cut on Wednesday furthered the gap between Israeli and U.S. interest rates, supporting a stronger shekel.   \par \par \uc2 On the crosses, the shekel traded at 3.4246 per 100 yen compared with 3.4439 on Wednesday, against the euro at 5.7155 from 5.7323 shekels and against the pound at 8.2459 from 8.2221.   \par \par \uc2 For further information double click on the following:   \par \par \uc2 <ILS=> for latest commercial bank shekel/dollar rates.   \par \par \uc2 <ILS1=> for a list of commercial bank shekel/dollar rates.   \par \par \uc2 <ILS2=> for a list of commercial bank shekel/basket rates.   \par \par \uc2 <BOIJ> for Bank of Israel rates.   \par \par \uc2 <FXIL> for Israeli spot and mid rates by the hour.   \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ISRAEL/SHEKEL|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|MD|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020071101e3b1001zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
