\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Bank of Israel seen cutting key rate to 3.75 pct   \b0\par\par\uc2 761 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2007\par 15:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * WHAT: Bank of Israel interest rate decision   \par \par \uc2 * WHEN: April 22, announcement due at 1530 GMT   \par \par \uc2 * Economists predict a quarter-point reduction in the key lending rate to 3.75 percent   \par \par \uc2 By Steven Scheer   \par \par \uc2 JERUSALEM, April 19 (Reuters) - Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably lower rates for the sixth time in seven months next week as the shekel's continued strength against the dollar keeps inflation below zero, a Reuters poll on Thursday showed.   \par \par \uc2 All but one of the 17 economists surveyed expect a rate cut on Sunday, a decision moved up by one day due to Israel's observance of Memorial Day on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Fourteen analysts forecast a quarter-point rate reduction, while two more predicted an even stronger half-point move, although some of those expecting a quarter-point believe there was a good chance of a half-point cut.   \par \par \uc2 "What he should do is 50 (basis points) but there was a hawkish atmosphere coming out of the most recent monetary meeting," said Jonathan Katz, economist at HSBC, referring to minutes showing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unanimous in supporting no move in late March.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts said they believe there will be a rate cut even though there should not be one since prior rate reductions have failed to halt the shekel from strengthening. One economist said there would not be a rate move.   \par \par \uc2 After five straight monthly rate reductions totalling 1.5 percentage points, the Bank of Israel held rates steady in late March on the view that previous rate cuts and a rapidly growing economy would eventually nudge inflation back up within the official annual target range of between 1 and 3 percent.   \par \par \uc2 At the time, the shekel had been hovering around the 4.20 per dollar range and many economists believed the current monetary easing cycle was over for the time being.   \par \par \uc2 SHEKEL AT 6-YEAR PEAK VS DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2 But after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to hold rates, the shekel took off, rallying this week to as much as 4.05 to the dollar, its strongest level since December 2000. On Thursday, dollar-shekel was at 4.08.   \par \par \uc2 With Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Stanley Fischer rejecting calls for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forced to use short-term interest rates as the main tool in trying to manipulate the exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 A strong shekel typically brings inflation down since housing -- more than 20 percent of the consumer price index -- and many imported goods such as fuel are linked to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Those costs have more than offset price gains in food and other items in Israel not influenced by the dollar since a strong economy has led to high consumer demand and spending.   \par \par \uc2 The consumer price index for March was 0.9 percent lower than a year earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Economists have revised 2007 inflation estimates to a rate of 0 to 1 percent, with inflation reaching the target in 2008.   \par \par \uc2 "Following the recent appreciation of the shekel...the Bank of Israel will be under significant pressure to cut rates further by at least 25 basis points, perhaps even 50 basis points," Reinhard Cluse, emerging markets economist at UBS, wrote in a report.   \par \par \uc2 "Clearly, the bank is aware of the risk that additional rate cuts might cause a more serious overheating a couple quarters from now," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Fischer himself last week said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in a tough spot regarding its interest rate policy.   \par \par \uc2 "We are in a situation where we have to consider how rapidly we lower the interest rate or at all because the economy is growing so fast," Fischer said, referring to an expected growth rate of about 5 percent for the fourth straight year.   \par \par \uc2 Bucking the trend of predicting a rate cut next week was Bank Leumi economist Gil Bufman. He echoed the views of others in saying the combination of rate cuts now plus a strong economy could pose trouble for the Bank of Israel next year.   \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Israel has been lowering the interest rate but the shekel is getting stronger," Bufman said, noting the shekel's strength was due to foreign investment flows that are not sensitive to interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 "At some stage, if he cuts more we may get into a problem like the build-vc'up of inflation pressures that may come in 2008 as well as a build-up of a financial bubble."   \par \par \uc2 ISRAEL-RATES/POLL|LANGEN|MD|ABN|M|D|E|RBN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020070419e34j001g2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
