\par\par \b \uc2 Israel's daily  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  trade volume $1.7 bln in 2003.   \b0\par\par\uc2 271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TEL AVIV, March 24 (Reuters) - Average daily trading volume in Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market reached $1.7 billion in 2003, up 10 percent from 2002, the Bank of Israel said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The growth is lowest since the bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed its policy and stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1997, a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said.   \par \par \uc2 In a statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the 10 percent growth rate was smaller than that of previous years. In 2002 the average daily volume grew 12 percent compared with the previous year, while in 2001 and 2000 volumes nearly doubled.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the lower volume growth could be explained by the fact that Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has reached maturity and is thus growing at a slower rate than in previous years. Or alternatively, the slow development of Israel's capital and money markets is hindering additional growth in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the bank said.   \par \par \uc2 "A faster development of the local markets could have increased their attractiveness to foreign investors and encouraged a rise in their activity in the local capital markets," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is highly centralised, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. Just a small number of market makers are active in this market, while 57 percent of the exchange activity is done by the three biggest Israeli banks and 83 percent by the five biggest banks.   \par \par \uc2 "Because of the high centralisation, very few market makers provide liquidity to the market, something that could hamper its development in the future," the statement said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040324e03o0020w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
