\par\par \b \uc2  Israeli Shekel Can Find Support In Tight Policy Stance \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amy Teibel \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 768 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2002\par 16:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Israel's decision to keep interest rates on hold is expected to have less of an impact on the shekel (ILS) than the security situation and an expected interest rate rise in May. \par \par \uc2  After the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced Monday that it was freezing the discount rate at 4.4% for April, the shekel slipped to ILS4.68 to the dollar from its ILS4.6650 Bank of Israel fixing an hour earlier. It then edged up, and traded between ILS4.6670 and ILS4.6780 through most of Tuesday before weakening to ILS4.6830 by 1530 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Short-term, the decision could weaken the shekel, Ilan Zadka, head of the corporate desk at the United Mizrahi Bank trading room, said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "It's less attractive," Zadka said. "If the rate had risen, then the shekel would have been more attractive." \par \par \uc2  Over the coming months, sources see the shekel trading within a range of ILS4.57-4.78 to the dollar and within a narrower range of 4.63-4.70 over the next few weeks, driven mainly by political developments. \par \par \uc2  "If there's no cease-fire, no good outcome from the cease-fire talks, from (U.S. envoy Anthony) Zinni, or from the Arab summit, there's no reason the dollar shouldn't go much higher...the 4.75 area again," said Michael Rabinowitz, a senior dealer at Israel Discount Bank. \par \par \uc2  "If there's some good news on the peace front, the dollar could go back down to the 4.62 level," Rabinowitz said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel will also be influenced by expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't maintain its neutral stance on the discount rate, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "You'd think logically that the dollar would rise and the shekel would fall," said Daniel Hass, head of institutional sales at the Bank Hapoalim trading room. \par \par \uc2  But over the past couple of months, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Klein has "littered the market with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and he may make some comment on the future possibility of rate hikes," Hass said. \par \par \uc2  Such hints on the Bank of Israel's part have strengthened the shekel - most recently a Feb. 12 warning that brought the shekel up from a ILS4.78 low and foreshadowed an actual rate hike in March. \par \par \uc2  Klein, in fact, suggested broadly in his rate announcement that further rises may be in the offing. Though he acknowledged that price surges in the first two months of the year wouldn't heat up annual inflation, he repeated that fiscal laxity would force him to tighten credit to meet the government's 2002 inflation target range of 2%-3%. \par \par \uc2  "Adhering to a budget framework that ensures low long-term interest rates and a declining government debt/GDP ratio is a necessary condition for maintaining price stability in the long run," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The government is widely expected to significantly overshoot its 2002 budget deficit target of 3% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Policy Reversal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised its base lending rate by 60 basis points for\par March to counteract the inflationary effect of a shekel that weakened\par 13% after a 200 basis point rate cut in late December.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Further rate rises are now expected to keep the shekel in check and\par suppress capital outflows. Analysts and market sources see the discount\par rate back up to 5%-5.5% by the end of the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think certainly if we go up to (ILS)4.75, we are going to hear Dr.\par Klein threatening to raise again," Rabinowitz said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hass expects Klein to raise the key lending rate if the U.S. starts\par hiking its own rates again as the economy recovers, reasoning that\par without the differential the shekel won't be attractive. Rabinowitz said\par an Israeli rate rise wouldn't necessarily follow from a U.S. hike -\par unless a devaluation follows, fueling inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "At the end of the day, Dr. Klein is looking where inflation is\par going," he said. "And if we're overshooting inflation then he'll raise\par the rates."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par An illiquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market through the end of the Jewish Passover\par holiday late next week will also leave its mark on the shekel in the\par immediate term, sources noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The holiday period is coming up, so no one is going to push it (the\par dollar) wildly, because liquidity is going to be really low for the next\par couple of days," Hass said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amy Teibel, Dow Jones Newswires; 972-2-537-6985;\par amy.teibel@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020326dy3q0055r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
