d a clear statement\par of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's mandate of preserving price stability.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market doesn't trade on Sunday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The draft legislation, which Cabinet is to discuss next Sunday, comes\par at a time when the economy is stagnating, the budget deficit is\par ballooning, and the Bank of Israel is considering raising rates again\par after a 200-basis-point cut fueled price rises.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Finance Minister Silvan Shalom, furious that the Dec. 25 rate cut was\par negotiated behind his back, is no less happy with the notion of rate\par rises, calling them a hindrance to growth and job creation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think that the government, and particularly the minister of\par finance, is worried that the economy is going to look bad and he's\par trying to clutch some straws to avoid sinking," the economist said. "And\par evidently those straws are in the form of irresponsible monetary policy\par and high inflation as something that is supposed to be a magic cure for\par GDP growth and unemployment."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The finance minister likely hopes the draft legislation will curb any\par planned rate rises in the short term, and in the longer term, "cause the\par Bank of Israel to be another department within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,"\par he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par If the government wants to administer monetary policy, "you don't need\par the Bank of Israel at all," a banking source said on condition of\par anonymity.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This would very much weaken the Bank of Israel," he said. "I'm\par certainly not enthusiastic about this."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - By Amy Teibel; Dow Jones Newswires; 972-2-537-6985;\par 