\par\par \b \uc2  Israel cbank mulls rates as CPI rises,economy dives. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Steven Scheer \par \uc2 878 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2002\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Feb 25 (Reuters) - Faced with a deepening recession and rising consumer prices, the Bank of Israel faces one of its toughest decisions ever on Monday on whether to raise interest rates to prevent a potential spike in inflation. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were unusually reluctant to predict which way \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Klein will jump when he announces the monetary programme later on Monday. \par \par \uc2  He could leave rates alone, or raise them anywhere from 0.2 to 1.0 point depending on how Klein views the dive in the shekel against the dollar - which has led to higher consumer prices - analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "He has a big dilemma. It's almost a lose-lose situation for him," said Tal Vardi, vice president at Citibank in Tel Aviv. \par \par \uc2  What is known is there will be no more rate reductions. A rate cut cycle that began in late 1999 seemingly culminated in December with an emphatic two-point drop in the key lending rate to an all-time low of 3.8 percent, meant to aid an economy hit by the global downturn aggravated by the Palestinian uprising. \par \par \uc2  Since then, two conflicting forces have emerged in the economy: a 10 percent drop in the shekel that sent housing and other items linked to the dollar soaring - reflected in a 1.1 percent jump in the consumer price index in January - but also data showing a worsening economy and unemployment above 10 percent for the first time since 1993. \par \par \uc2  "There's a recession going on which we saw in unemployment and GDP (gross domestic product) numbers," said Ptachia Bar-Shavit, chief economist at Bank Hapoalim. "There are very few signs of a recovery. If there is a recovery, it is very fragile." \par \par \uc2  Bank Hapoalim, which initially gave even odds for a rate hike, on Sunday offered a prediction of a one percentage point increase. \par \par \uc2  Economists said an interest rate increase at this time would squash any rebound and counter any positive effects the economy would gain from cheaper exports. Business leaders have also urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold rates. \par \par \uc2  "Klein (shouldn't) raise interest rates when we have a 10.2 percent unemployment rate," said Shlomo Maoz, chief economist at Nessuah Zannex Securities. \par \par \uc2  Still, a decision to take no action could weaken the shekel further and fuel more CPI gains, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has priced in at least a half-point rate increase based on Klein's warning two weeks ago that he would not tolerate inflation. \par \par \uc2  SHEKEL GAINS ON RATE HIKE SPECULATION \par \par \uc2  On Monday morning the shekel was gaining 0.9 percent against the dollar as the market speculated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise its key lending rate by more than a half-point. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's representative rate on Friday was set at 4.70 against the dollar, near its weakest ever point reached earlier this month. After the shekel depreciated by more than 10 percent in two months Klein warned of a rate hike and the shekel temporarily calmed. \par \par \uc2  "I really believe Klein doesn't want to hike rates because the economy is doing poorly and (a hike) would send a bad signal from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it is doing a zigzag" with rates, Vardi said. "I would wait another month at least." \par \par \uc2  Economists predicted a CPI rise of more than one percent in February as effects of the shekel drop continue to feed into the economy, followed by a 0.6 percent rise in March. \par \par \uc2  After that, many analysts believe prices will stabilise, but expect inflation to be more than four percent this year - above the government's 2-3 percent target rate. Economic growth is forecast at less than 1.0 percent after a 0.5 percent contraction in 2001 and some analysts fear stagflation. \par \par \uc2  "Prices are going up but it's not inflation; a one-time price adjustment is not inflation," said Vardi. \par \par \uc2  "But if Klein feels it's the beginning of an inflation cycle or feels there will be a run on the shekel he will raise rates," he said. "If he feels the price rises will only be in January, February and March because of the depreciation of the shekel he will not change rates." \par \par \uc2  Maoz, though, was confident Klein would hike rates at least a half-point even though he did not believe rate should rise. \par \par \uc2  "If he doesn't raise rates, the depreciation in the shekel will go to five to the dollar and people will say he is not fighting inflation," he said. "If he doesn't raise it by a half-point now, he will have to raise rates 1-1.5 points in one shot later." \par \par \uc2  "By March, we will be at three percent inflation," he said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and sell dollars rather than raise rates to stem the shekel's weakness. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing the market is too large to fight. \par \par \uc2  "They have $23 billion of reserves," Maoz said. "Central banks hold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for emergency times and this is an emergency. If you don't use it in an emergency, then when will you use it?" \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020225dy2p00i7q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
