\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Israel shekel slide hits 10 pct vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Steven Scheer \par \uc2 956 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Februar 2002\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Feb 11 (Reuters) - Israel's shekel, down 10 percent since a steep interest rate cut in late December, fell to new lows against the dollar on Monday, sparking fears of renewed inflation. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's representative rate was set at 4.7440, down 1.6 percent from Friday's level of 4.6670. \par \par \uc2  The dive in the shekel comes against the backdrop of a reeling Israeli economy that has been hit hard by a global economic downturn and a Palestinian uprising that has lasted 16 months, hurting exports and keeping tourists away. \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product (GDP) growth is expected at 0.5-2.0 percent in 2002 after a 0.5 percent decline in 2001. \par \par \uc2  "It's a one-way market right now," said Gideon Sadeh, head of Treasury sales at HSBC in Tel Aviv. "You don't have any dollar sellers." \par \par \uc2  The rapid descent of the shekel, which had remained strong against the dollar since the end of 1999, has particularly raised inflation fears since housing prices are linked to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Inflation, which was just 1.4 percent in 2001 is seen rising to around three percent in 2002. \par \par \uc2  But the shekel's fall has also given hope to exporters who have been hard hit due to the combination of a global economic slump and a resilient Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the last three years. \par \par \uc2  "That will stimulate the economy, but if the shekel continues to depreciate the Bank of Israel will have no other choice but to raise interest rates," said Yacov Sheinin, president of the Economic Models consultancy. "The only way to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to raise interest rates." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by buying shekels, since it believes the global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is too large to fight. \par \par \uc2  But raising rates would be an about-face, since it was a steep rate cut in late December that ignited the shekel's dive. Bank of Israel Governor David Klein on December 23 slashed the country's key lending rate by two percentage points to an all-time low of 3.8 percent as part of a deal in which the Finance Ministry agreed to chop government spending in 2002. \par \par \uc2  "It's not so bad to have a weak shekel but it's the way it was done," Sheinin said, adding that Klein should have lowered rates in steps in order to monitor the effects of the cut. "Now we are paying the price. This is what we call zig-zag policy." \par \par \uc2  SMALL RATE CUTS \par \par \uc2  Since the end of 1999, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut the lending rate in gradual increments of 0.2 to 0.3 percentage point at a time from a rate of 11.5 percent as inflation was benign. The bank repeatedly warned of a volatile reaction from financial markets if interest rates fell too fast too soon. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the high level of interest rates and a moderate rate differential between Israel and the United States helped support the shekel until almost the end of 2001, even as the Israeli economy entered a recession. \par \par \uc2  With only a 2.05 point differential in rates between Israel and the United States, analysts said more of a premium is now required to hold shekels given the upheaval in the economic and security situations and as many believe U.S. rates have bottomed out with the federal funds rate at 1.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The shekel hit close to 4.40 to the dollar in late 1998 during the collapse of emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  But it strengthened back to near 4.0 to the dollar after the Bank of Israel raised interest rates by 4.0 percentage points in late 1998 to curb inflation and steady the shekel. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the shekel was set at 3.5411 shekels per 100 yen compared with 3.4905, against the euro at 4.1690 shekels against 4.0738 and against the pound at 6.7531 shekels versus 6.5933. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Monday's sharp drop in the shekel is the result of market perception that the Bank of Israel is not resisting the development. Escalating Israeli-Palestinian violence was also pressuring the shekel. \par \par \uc2  "The market feels that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market so there is a feeling of further appreciation in the dollar," said a dealer at Bank Hapoalim. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that much of the shekel's drop has come from the general public selling shekel assets through mutual funds. \par \par \uc2  "People have been waiting for the dollar to retrace lower so they can buy at cheaper levels," said the Hapoalim dealer. "But a lot of people missed the opportunity to buy at lower levels and now don't want to miss any further (shekel) depreciation." \par \par \uc2  Still, dealers are unwilling to speculate how much further the shekel will weaken since analysts had bet on a level of no more than 4.75 to the dollar by the end of the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar broke through resistance levels without any difficulties," noted HSBC's Sadeh. \par \par \uc2  The near-term direction of the shekel depends, dealers agreed, on Friday's release of the consumer price index (CPI) for January which will indicate the impact of its slide. \par \par \uc2  If the CPI comes in as expected at a rise of no more than 0.8 percent and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds rates steady, "fixed players will think they have another month to play with until the February CPI and the dollar can reach higher levels," Sadeh said. "But the only thing that will derail the dollar's rise is a rate increase." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020211dy2b00r7t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
