\par\par \b \uc2  Israel's Concern Is Shekel's Strength, Not Vulnerability \b0\par\par\uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2001\par 17:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Jennifer Downey \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to tumble in times of political turmoil or war, but no one seems to be taking that bet with the Israeli shekel. \par \par \uc2  Since the beginning 14 months ago of the second intifada, or Palestinian uprising, the shekel has declined a mere five percent, despite suicide bombings, a rise in terrorist-group popularity and a shift in  the Israeli government. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, many analysts have expressed concern that squeezing Palestinian leader Yasir Arafat out of power will produce a void that may allow dangerous factions to gain influence. After another terrorist attack Wednesday, Israeli armed forces retaliated and the administration of Prime Minister Ariel Sharon cut off ties with Arafat. \par \par \uc2  But rather than fearing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fallout during the political crisis, some analysts are more concerned about an overly strong shekel during worldwide economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  Despite the deteriorating geopolitical backdrop, the dollar traded around ILS4.2250 on Wednesday and is trading only slightly stronger Thursday at ILS4.2270. \par \par \uc2  "If you look back two years, including terrorist attacks or other turmoil, it did nothing except provide intraday volatility" in the dollar/shekel exchange rate, said Joseph Barnea, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Bank Leumi in New York. "I have to say, it's steady like a rock." \par \par \uc2  The same phenomenon can be seen in Israeli bond spreads, which haven't widened much in light of the traumatic events of the past two weeks, said Assaf Gutterman, chief economist at the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mission to New York. \par \par \uc2  A chief reason for predictable moves in the shekel is the "clockwork" series of month-end conversions, when Israeli companies transfer dollars and euros into shekel, Barnea said. \par \par \uc2  Israel is heavily reliant on foreign markets, mainly in the U.S. and Europe, and demand abroad has helped drive Israel's competitive high-technology industry. Thus the regular transactions these companies require in order to move assets from international sales back into Israel  provide a consistent source of shekel buying. \par \par \uc2  So far, whenever speculative traders have dabbled in the shekel, "commercial interests have somehow been able to put a lid on everything," Barnea said. "Those trying over time to sell shekels and buy dollars on political events or security issues have been smacked." \par \par \uc2  But companies are actually being harmed by a general propensity among Israelis to stay in the shekel instead of expanding into assets denominated in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Joseph Morgenstern, consultant and publisher of the Israel High-Tech & Investment Report. \par \par \uc2  Israel's key lending rate is 5.8%, over four hundred basis points higher than the current U.S. federal funds rate. Rates offered for bank savings in Israel are around 5.5% and some Israeli government bonds yield a 9.7% tax-free return, Morgenstern said, providing strong reinforcement for citizens to keep their money in shekels. \par \par \uc2  This leads to "what's euphemistically called a mountain of shekels," which sounds positive but is dangerous for Israeli companies which are already suffering from the global economic slowdown, he said. \par \par \uc2  A high-priced shekel reduces the competitiveness of Israeli exports. The problem is exacerbated because a large amount of Israel's exports are in the high-tech sector, which has struggled after the collapse of the late 1990s bubble economy. In return, imports are cheaper, further reducing the domestic market for Israeli goods. \par \par \uc2  Far from fearing that political consequences will take a toll on the shekel, Morgenstern would welcome a decline in the shekel's exchange rate in order to stave off dire economic consequences. \par \par \uc2  "I think a country of six million people has to maximize the return on its exports. It needs to have a rational exchange rate," Morgenstern said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel trades within a 36% band, and each trading day the Bank of Israel assigns a "fixed" rate for the shekel against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the shekel is free to trade at any rate within the wide band. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but as concerns over the strength of the shekel grow, it may become more difficult for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Barnea said. \par \par \uc2  Early this week, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Klein sharply addressed this concern, warning that Israel's exchange rate policy could "explode in our face one day," and suggesting that the band should be scrapped in order to avoid the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Like the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime concern is price stability. The Bank of Israel, like the ECB, has heard more voices calling for the Bank of Israel to make more drastic interest-rate cuts to spur growth during the global slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Because the interest-rate incentive is a key reason the shekel is so strong, a reduction in these rates would not only weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but make the economic downturn less taxing on exporters and likely even boost employment, Gutterman said. \par \par \uc2  But in the meantime, turmoil in the Middle East is "calculated in the risk" that is inherent in the high rates that Israeli assets offer, and when people buy shekels, they already know this, Gutterman said. \par \par \uc2  "The international community understands the situation that Israel is in," he said. "They understand they're not buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Netherlands." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Jennifer Downey, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2019;\par jennifer.downey@dowjones.com\par -0-\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In contrast, the South African rand, which suffers from social and\par political unrest in neighboring Zimbabwe, has fallen around 37% within\par the same time period.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011213dxcd008sr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
