\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Of Israel Head Urges Pension Reform,End To Shekel Band \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2001\par 14:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  CAESAREA, Israel -(Dow Jones)- Bank of Israel Governor David Klein took advantage of Israel's most important economic conference Thursday to demand deep reforms to the country's pension system, and an end to the shekel trading band and the cap on treasury bill sales. \par \par \uc2  Klein urged that the pension system be forced off its diet of non-tradable government bonds and gradually move to market instruments, to help the domestic capital market. \par \par \uc2  "Without a deep and fluid bond market, the stock market can't develop either, and that clearly hurts the economy's ability to grow," Klein told a conference of leading figures in government and the private sector. \par \par \uc2  Conceding that it would be unrealistic to undertake a wholesale reform of the pension sector, Klein urged a partial reform that would bar new members to the funds from having their pensions financed by non-traded bonds. \par \par \uc2  He said tax benefits accruing to pension holders should be awarded according to the taxpayer's income level rather than across the board. \par \par \uc2  The governor, facing off against the resistant Finance Ministry, repeated his call to eliminate the shekel trading band. He said hyperinflation and other conditions that once made it necessary no longer exist, and warned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could one day be forced either to jack up interest rates to defend the band or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "If we are forced to defend (the band), then we will ultimately be forced to eliminate it, after paying a high and unnecessary price," Klein cautioned. "Better to do it at our own initiative and not under market pressure." \par \par \uc2  The last time the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market, between 1995 and 1997, it bought $16.5 billion, he noted. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry objects to eliminating the shekel band, on the grounds that a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depreciate. \par \par \uc2  Klein also took the Finance Ministry to task for capping sales of treasury bills, saying it prevents the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from using them as a monetary tool. Left only with interest rates, Klein said the bank was compelled to keep them slightly higher than necessary. \par \par \uc2  He said stability of asset prices, like securities and real estate, were a key concern of policy-makers. But he warned that without reform of the pension system, the trading band and T-bill sales, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in a poor position to ensure stability of asset prices. \par \par \uc2  "Only after these constraints are lifted and the culture of price stability becomes firmly rooted, can the Bank of Israel turn its attention to the financial markets, in an effort to contribute to the preservation of their stability," Klein said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amy Teibel, Dow Jones Newswires; 972-2-537-6985;\par amy.teibel@dowjones.com\par -0- 21/06/01 12-28G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6l02w1n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
