\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel says no need to impact shekel trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 16:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, March 25 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees no need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in or influence shekel trade despite a recent depreciation, as long as the weakening does not translate into higher inflation, the spokesman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  The spokesman's office confirmed remarks by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Klein published Sunday in the Maariv newspaper. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as long as the depreciation does not threaten meeting the inflation targets and price stability," Klein said. "The shekel's depreciation is positive as long as it is not accompanied by higher prices." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman's office said Klein's remarks did not refer to direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but to the use of monetary policy as an influence on shekel trade. \par \par \uc2  The shekel has slipped more than 4 percent against the dollar since the start of 2001 as international markets have been rattled by a Wall Street purge and Israeli high-tech companies find their resources for importing capital are drying up. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Israeli inflation is forecast at 1.5-2 percent for 2001, well below a government target of 2.5-3.5 percent for the year and matching a long-term goal to stay in a range of 1-3 percent inflation in line with Western economies. \par \par \uc2  Klein reiterated his view that the shekel's trading band - a mechanism that defines limits for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation or appreciation before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must defend it - should be removed. \par \par \uc2  "The trading band ... is artificial and unnecessary, and no one would feel it if we got rid of it," he told Maariv. \par \par \uc2  Israel's Finance Ministry has opposed removing the band, fearing the lack of a defined limit could encourage too rapid a rise or fall in the shekel's value. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains that the shekel's value should be determined by the market alone and that monetary policy is the proper tool for influencing the market. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 4.22 shekels). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3p03h7p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
