\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Shekel Topping 4-to-Dlr; Grazing Strong Pt In Band \b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2000\par 08:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM -(Dow Jones)- The shekel peaked above four to the dollar early Thursday for the first time since late March, grazing the strongest point in its trading band and taunting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  All this is happening amid a fierce dispute between the Finance Ministry and the Bank of Israel over whether to abolish the shekel trading band. \par \par \uc2  At 0545 GMT, the average between ask and bid prices at several Israeli banks reached ILS3.9990. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is about 1.5% below the strongest point in its trading band. Should it strengthen beyond that point, then the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the shekel and force it back within the confines of its band. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor David Klein has been pushing since his appointment in January for the elimination of the band, arguing that it is no longer needed given Israel's low inflation and liberalized foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Inflation for all of 2000 is projected at some 1.6%. \par \par \uc2  But the band can't be jettisoned without the support of the Finance Ministry, which argues that instead of a freely floating shekel, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should cut the discount rate instead. The discount rate for September has been cut 20 basis points to 8.9%, but inflation-adjusted rates continue to hover in the 8% range. \par \par \uc2  Lower rates would make foreign inflows less attractive and ease upward pressure on the shekel, the ministry reasons. They would also help old economy sectors that are largely responsible for Israel's 8.5% unemployment rate, and whose exports struggle to compete when the shekel is strong. \par \par \uc2  The Ha'aretz newspaper reported Thursday that the Finance Ministry has proposed that the trading band be phased out by 2003, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it didn't know of any such plan. \par \par \uc2  The shekel is adjusted daily against a basket of four \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated by the dollar, and including the British pound, the euro and the Japanese yen. It is allowed to fluctuate 15% on either side of its midpoint. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been strengthening steadily throughout August because of large capital inflows to Israeli high-tech companies, export growth and foreign investment in infrastructure projects. Political instability has also strengthened the shekel by delaying progress on a proposed tax overhaul that eliminates tax disincentives for investing in foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  Some economists say the band should be scuttled when Israel implements the tax reforms. The reforms would encourage capital outflows, easing the upward pressure on the shekel allaying Finance Ministry fears that the shekel could continue to strengthen if the band is jettisoned, they said. \par \par \uc2  -By Amy Teibel, Dow Jones Newswires; 972-2-537-6985; amy.teibel@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8v049dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
