\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  head says will  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if needed. \b0\par\par\uc2 179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2000\par 09:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Aug 23 (Reuters) - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor David Klein said on Wednesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade if the shekel hit the strongest limit in its trading band. \par \par \uc2  "If and when the exchange rate will hit the bottom part of the band, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because this is our obligation," Klein told \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Army Radio. \par \par \uc2  Klein called on the government this week to eliminate the trading band - a mechanism that allows the shekel to fluctuate 17 percent above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint - before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary. \par \par \uc2  Klein said that although the exact timing depends on the market, "the moment will definitely come in which the exchange rate hits the lower limit of the trading band. And so it will be necessary to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will needlessly embroil the economy." \par \par \uc2  The shekel is currently 2 percent away from its strongest point in the band, but Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Barak's government has yet to show support for abolishing it. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8n0aqao\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
