\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Discount Rate Seen Falling Under 9% In Sept \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2000\par 14:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  By Amy Teibel \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Israel is expected to announce an interest rate cut for the third month in a row on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , under its governor David Klein, has cut the rate from 9.5% in June to its current 9.1% and is expected to cut the September rate by a further 20 to 30 basis points. \par \par \uc2  Economists at Israel Discount Bank forecast a 30 basis point reduction, noting that inflation expectations 12 months out, as measured by yields in the bond market, dropped to some 1.5% in the first half of August. That's significantly lower than the government's 2001 inflation rate target of 2% to 3%. Inflation for all of 2000 is seen at 1.5% to 2%, below the government's target of 3% to 4%. \par \par \uc2  The moderate 10.3% rise on the year in the M1 narrow money supply as of July, and the strengthening of the shekel against the dollar, also support a continued reduction in the discount rate, the economists said. \par \par \uc2  Jonathan Katz, an economist at Nessuah Zannex Securities Ltd. in Tel Aviv, forecasts a 20 basis point reduction for September, and an additional cut of 40 points cut by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  "I think probably the fact that the U.S. didn't change their rates will make it a little bit easier for Klein although, even without that, the underlying inflationary environment here is very low, so I think he can continue to lower rates," Katz said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. rate rises sometimes tame enthusiasm for Israeli rate cuts, because a shrinking gap between U.S. and Israeli rates traditionally feeds demand for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Shekel Well Bid\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the shekel has strengthened significantly in recent weeks on\par foreign inflows to Israel's high-tech sector, foreign investment in\par Israeli infrastructure and export growth. Political instability has also\par strengthened the shekel because it has delayed progress on a proposed\par tax reform plan that supports a weaker shekel by making foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par investments more attractive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  went as high as ILS4.0240 to the dollar at the Bank of\par Israel fixing Tuesday, compared with ILS4.1050 in late July, though it\par weakened to ILS4.0380 at Wednesday's fixing on the U.S. rate decision.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As it approaches its strongest point in its trading band, the Bank of\par Israel continues to push to have the band eliminated. Klein says the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can be allowed to float freely because inflation is low and\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy has been significantly liberalized.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the band can't be scuttled without the support of the Finance\par Ministry, which argues that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should cut interest rates\par instead. Lower rates would make foreign inflows less attractive and ease\par upward pressure on the shekel, the ministry reasons. They would also\par help 'old economy' sectors that are the major contributors to Israel's\par 8.5% unemployment rate, and whose exports are hard-pressed to compete\par when the shekel is strong.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "One reason the shekel is approaching the ceiling of its trading band\par is that interest rates are high relative to those in Western economies,"\par the Finance Ministry said in a release Wednesday. "Measured and prudent\par discount rate cuts would halt the appreciation of the shekel and\par eliminate the need (for Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Israel\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par trading."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market to defend the trading band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some economists say the band should be dumped when Israel implements\par the tax reforms, which eliminate the tax disincentives for investing in\par foreign assets. This would ease the upward pressure on the shekel and\par encourage capital outflows, allaying Finance Ministry fears that the\par shekel could continue to strengthen if the band is jettisoned, they say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Israel Discount Bank economists see the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthening to\par ILS4 to the dollar in coming weeks. Katz believes it could go even\par higher if there is a breakthrough in Mideast peacemaking. But he sees it\par weakening to ILS4.10 or ILS4.15 in 2001 on the back of continued Israeli\par rate cuts and U.S. rate rises.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amy Teibel, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 972-2-537-6985;amy.teibel@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw8n043s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
