\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Israel Bank head says shekel band an impediment. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michele Gershberg \par \uc2 573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2000\par 14:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Aug 20 (Reuters) - Bank of Israel Governor David Klein said on Sunday that the shekel's eight-year-old trading band could endanger the country's inflation target and called for its elimination while the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is calm. \par \par \uc2  Israel's shekel is allowed to trade some 17 percent above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committed to defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  once it reaches the upper or lower limits of the band. \par \par \uc2  In his first interview with the international media since taking office in January, Klein told Reuters that Israel's approach towards Western inflation levels could bring the shekel to its strongest point of the band. The shekel is currently less than three percentage points away from that strongpoint. \par \par \uc2  "A day will come, and I don't think it's very far away, where the actual exchange rate will reach the limit of the band just because we don't have any more inflation differentials" with other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel has strengthened to a level of 4.04 to the dollar compared with 4.15 at the beginning of the year, largely due to an influx of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  invested in Israeli high-tech companies and accelerated economic growth. \par \par \uc2  In addition, the shekel's strongpoint on the trading band is built to depreciate at a rate of two percent annually to account for the inflation differential between Israel and developed countries. \par \par \uc2  But with Israeli inflation expected to finish below 3 percent in 2000, that differential is becoming insignificant and could bring the shekel even closer to its limit, he said. \par \par \uc2  Once that happens, the Bank of Israel would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the shekel and as a result, "pour shekels into the economy, increase the means of payment and endanger the inflation target," he said. \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, "in order to absorb those shekels, we will have to raise interest rates" to stave off the inflationary pressures, he said. "This is why I think the band is really an impediment now." \par \par \uc2  FOREIGNERS TO LOOK ASKANCE AT CENBANK \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Klein said that for the past three years \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had an opportunity to abolish the trading band and he most recently called on the government to do so last week during a vote on the 2001 national budget. \par \par \uc2  "We should do it when the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is calm," Klein said. "It's not any particular day or month. It's whenever we are within the band and we don't have to defend it - this is a good opportunity." \par \par \uc2  Klein does not expect the band's abolition under such circumstances would shake up the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market which has been determining the shekel's exchange rate according to its own needs for several years. \par \par \uc2  However, if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in trading - something not seen since late 1997 - the move could have a negative effect on foreign investors active in the market. \par \par \uc2  "They are not going to understand if we start entering as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...the market in order to protect artificially a given trend of exchange rates," he said. \par \par \uc2  "This is going to change all their views of the Israeli market. This is also a reason why we should be a lot more careful about moving away this threat of the existing band." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8k0avyj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
