\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Lebanese gloomy as Israeli withdrawal nears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jack Redden \par \uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2000\par 16:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BEIRUT, April 18 (Reuters) - A sense of foreboding is settling over Lebanon as a long-sought end to Israel's occupation of the south threatens to bring war rather than peace. \par \par \uc2  "Anyone who really believes that an Israeli pullback will automatically herald the end of Lebanon's problems is in for a rude awakening," The Daily Star newspaper said in an editorial on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  That pullback - and the unpredictable consequences it will unleash - took on a feeling of inevitability with Israel's formal notification to the United Nations on Monday that it would end its 22-year occupation by July. \par \par \uc2  But Lebanese are already showing deepening unease that they will be caught up in a power struggle between Syria and \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week to support the Lebanese pound for the first time in a year. People in south Lebanon, nearest the conflict, report some shops are running down their stocks. \par \par \uc2  The security problem is compounding already deep economic trouble. Earlier this month the Beirut stock exchange, shrinking since 1997, sank to a daily volume of a mere 1,046 shares worth $5,843. \par \par \uc2  Investment, such as a Four Seasons Hotel to be built by Prince Alwaleed bin Talal, the Saudi investor, has apparently been put on hold. Bankers say even heavy discounting cannot attract buyers for real estate acquired as collateral. \par \par \uc2  The atmosphere is more of resignation than panic; people still hope to avoid a major conflict and talk of Lebanon's resilience. But for a country rising from the ashes of the 1975-90 civil war, the prospect of an indefinite delay in Arab-Israeli peace and the investment that would bring is suffocating. \par \par \uc2  Lebanon and its dominant partner Syria have warned Israel that a withdrawal that is not part of a broad peace agreement returning Syria's Golan Heights will not give security to Israel, implying that attacks over the border will follow. \par \par \uc2  Israel has responded with threats of massive retaliation on Lebanon, which has served as the battleground between its more powerful neighbours for the past two decades. \par \par \uc2  JUSTIFYING FIGHTING \par \par \uc2  Both sides appear to be laying the groundwork for justifying fighting after a withdrawal. \par \par \uc2  Lebanon and Syria continue - despite Israel saying it will withdraw to borders recognised by the United Nations - to suggest that Israel will occupy small parts of Lebanon, which would be a cause for continued guerrilla attacks. \par \par \uc2  Lebanese Prime Minister Selim al-Hoss told members of parliament on Monday night, immediately after Israel's withdrawal announcement, that Lebanon could not be held responsible for border incidents. \par \par \uc2  But it will be. Israel has spent the weeks before the formal withdrawal announcement ensuring there will be no criticism from Washington and most other governments if it launches intense attacks on Lebanon in reprisal for even minor cross-border incidents. \par \par \uc2  A diplomatic source close to the process expected that Israel would quickly escalate any incident, hitting Lebanese infrastructure and even some of the 35,000 Syrian forces inside Lebanon. It is not to the advantage of Israel, with overwhelming military superiority in the region, to be drawn into a protracted conflict along the border. \par \par \uc2  Either conflict - short and highly destructive, or continuous clashes inflicting a steady toll - would be disastrous for Lebanon's fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Only strong economic growth can save the government from disappearing into a debt trap; debt servicing takes more than 80 percent of revenues and public debt is growing about 1.5 percent a month. The economy was stagnant last year and hopes for any substantial improvement depend on peace. \par \par \uc2  Increasingly, Lebanese professionals who returned to their country after the chaos of the civil war are questioning their prospects in a country where the hopes of the mid-1990s are now seen to be a mirage. \par \par \uc2  "I have a young family and I have to wonder what future there is for them here," said a professor who returned to help restore the American University of Beirut but is now contemplating leaving. \par \par \uc2  The government was swift to deny a report in As Safir newspaper this month of alarming levels of emigration. But the fact that the issue has moved on to the frontpages was a sure sign of the public mood. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4i026kj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
