\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  industry official urges cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Michele Gershberg \par \uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2000\par 15:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, April 10 (Reuters) - Israeli industry official Rimon Ben-Shaoul called on Monday for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading and stem the tide of capital inflows strengthening the shekel. Ben-Shaoul is head of the economic division at the Manufacturers Association of Israel and president of Clal Industries & Investments, the investment arm of one of Israel's largest conglomerates, Clal Israel. Ben-Shaoul said capital inflows surged to an annual rate of $7.6 billion in the first quarter versus some $4.5 billion in 1999, fuelled by funds raised and repatriated by the country's high-tech sector. \par \par \uc2  "There is an unusual situation of one-sided foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows leading to a real (shekel) appreciation, harming export profitability and unemployment," Ben-Shaoul told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "I think the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  must \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and stabilise it because this trend will continue and worsen," he said, estimating the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to buy several billion dollars of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  annually "in excess of the market's equilibrium." \par \par \uc2  Left untouched, a too-strong shekel would push unemployment as high as 9.8 percent in 2001 from 8.9 percent in 1999 and keep economic growth on a low burner at 3.2 percent annually, he said. A shekel-dollar rate of 4.2-4.25, as seen in 1999, would better serve Israeli exporters, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the shekel hits the upper or lower limit of its trading band, which allows the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate 18 percent above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint. \par \par \uc2  Ben-Shaoul advocated abolishing the band altogether, calling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategically. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  is supposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if we reach the (shekel's strongest) limit, but by the time we get there we'll have 250,000 unemployed people," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy has become ineffective against the appreciation because the market already factored in rate cuts, he said, adding "the Bank of Israel doesn't have a lot more room to cut rates - 1.5 to 2.0 percent." \par \par \uc2  Ben-Shaoul also called on the government to increase growth inducing investment and said he supported a reduced tax burden "which can only be accomplished with a tax on capital", among other measures. \par \par \uc2  ($1 = 4.03 shekels). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw4a02hyq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
