\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Ctrl Bk Must Cap Shekel, Say Manufacturers \b0\par\par\uc2 431 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2000\par 14:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM -(Dow Jones)- Israel's manufacturers urged the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday buy billions of dollars to reverse the shekel's rising trend, which it says is damaging exports and jobs. \par \par \uc2  Rimon Ben-Shaoul, chairman of the economics panel of the Manufacturers Association of Israel, blamed upward pressure on the shekel on capital inflows. \par \par \uc2  He also urged the government to more than double its infrastructure investments to $2 billion annually, and to cut taxes to the point that the budget deficit will widen. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading only to defend the trading band. It last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early 1998. \par \par \uc2  The shekel (ILS) has strengthened to some ILS4.03 to the dollar from some ILS4.2800 at the end of September, largely on the back of capital inflows in search of high-tech investments. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refused to comment on Ben-Shaoul's proposal. \par \par \uc2  Ben-Shaoul, who is chief executive of Clal Industries and Investments Ltd., acknowledged that his call for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was "unmodern and anachronistic." But he projected that foreigners attracted by Israeli high-tech industries would invest $7.6 billion in the country's industry in 2000, a rise of $3 billion over 1999. \par \par \uc2  These inflows cause the shekel to appreciate, making exports less profitable and causing unemployment to rise, he said. If the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to eliminate the shekel trading band and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, he said, then the economy could grow between 4% and 5%. \par \par \uc2  Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the economy will expand 3.2% in 2001 and unemployment will rise to 9.8%, he said. Joblessness in January was 9.3%. \par \par \uc2  The large capital inflows aren't "a natural economic development," and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should act now to offset its effects, he said. \par \par \uc2  Ben-Shaoul said he didn't know exactly how much foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Bank of Israel should buy, but said it should be "several billions." He said he'd like to see the shekel trading around ILS4.20 to ILS4.25 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On lower taxes, Ben-Shaoul said studies in the U.S. have shown that\par even if tax cuts aren't balanced in the budget, they still stimulate\par growth by themselves, thereby\par narrowing the deficit.   Ben-Shaoul also called for the introduction of\par capital gains tax on Israeli securities, which at present are not\par taxed. Previous efforts to impose a tax have failed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Finance Ministry-appointed panel is to submit a tax reform program\par in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw4a01tw2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
