\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Report Urges Israel To Ease Monetary Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 1998\par 12:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund urged Israeli economic authorities to establish conditions to gradually ease Israel's monetary policy and appeared to criticize the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  for not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading to calm market volatility. \par \par \uc2  The IMF also cautioned authorities against raising the government's 1999 inflation target of 4.0% and widening the 1999 budget deficit target of 2.0% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "A protracted period of high real interest rates would further weaken domestic demand," the IMF wrote in its preliminary annual report on the Israeli economy, published Wednesday. "Thus, economic policies in the next few months should strive to establish the conditions for a gradual easing of the monetary stance, without risking a rekindling of inflation or the re-emergence of pressure for a depreciation of the shekel." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel raised its key lending rate by four percentage points in November to 13.5% to curb higher inflation expectations stemming from the shekel's 17% depreciation between early August and late October. Annual 1998 inflation, which had appeared to be heading for just 4.0%, may be as high as 7.0% to 9.0% because the shekel weakened so sharply amid turmoil in international financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely criticized in Israel for not selling dollars in early October to calm the volatile market. The IMF report appeared to contain a veiled criticism of that stance. \par \par \uc2  "In pursuing monetary policy goals, it is desirable to avoid as far as possible abrupt changes in monetary conditions," the report said. "In this context, it would be useful to continue to explore whether limited \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market can be undertaken without unduly encouraging risky behavior." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 03-12-98 \par \par \uc2  1147GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duc301og9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
