\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel head under attack from old allies. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 713 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1998\par 16:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The head of Israel's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jacob Frenkel, is coming under heavy fire from old allies in the government and financial markets for his handling of the shekel's slide and resulting inflation in the last seven weeks. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think we needed to have three percent inflation and a 22 percent depreciation. He should have taken drastic steps earlier on," said Micha Goldberg, head of research at Nessuah Zannex Securities. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor was widely criticised for refusing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in early October, when the shekel tumbled against the dollar. After recovering some of its losses, it is down nearly 19 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. \par \par \uc2  Now he is being attacked for raising interest rates excessively. In less than three weeks, the Bank of Israel has raised the base monetary tender rate by four percentage points to an annual 13.5 percent in response to surging inflation. \par \par \uc2  October's consumer price index jumped 3.0 percent on the back of the shekel depreciation, the highest monthly index in more than seven years. But most economists say inflation will retreat to its pre-depreciation levels very quickly. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel's stubborn drive to bring down Israeli inflation to Western levels long ago earned him the disapproval of many business leaders and legislators. They blame the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tight monetary policy for the slowing economy and rising joblessness. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the head of the leading business lobby, the Manufacturers Association, called for the state comptroller to investigate monetary policy. In parliament, legislators said they were reviving efforts to create a board to oversee the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Those critics have been joined some of Frenkel's old supporters. \par \par \uc2  On Sunday, Finance Minister Yaacov Neeman said for the first time he has differences of opinion with Frenkel, although he refused to detail them. \par \par \uc2  Newspaper reports have said Neeman viewed Frenkel's last boost in interest rates - a two percentage point hike announced last Thursday - as wrong, as was the bank's refusal to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Neeman is expected to come back to the cabinet next week with plans to raise the government's inflation target for next year, a move Frenkel has opposed. \par \par \uc2  Ministers are seeking to boost the target, on the basis of which the Bank of Israel sets interest rates, to a range of four to six percent. The target is now at four percent. \par \par \uc2  "I think the party responsible for monetary policy must do some soul-searching with regard to the policy it is implementing," Treasury director-general Ben-Zion Zilberfarb told reporters on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Market economists have also been mainly critical of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Victor Shohet, chief economist at Ofek Securities, said Frenkel's orginal sin was cutting rates 1.5 points last August, a move that contributed to the shekel's first losses. \par \par \uc2  When the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began plunging in October, he acted too cautiously and then moved precipitously with two unscheduled rate hike announcements. \par \par \uc2  "He should have acted earlier (during the depreciation), but he tried to convey business as usual. Then he panicked," said Shohet, who said he nevertheless thought Frenkel had no choice but to jack up rates sharply. \par \par \uc2  Ed Butchart, an economist for Merrill Lynch in London, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had erred in not selling dollars to moderate the shekel's losses in October, thereby causing dollar-denominated housing prices to soar and raising inflation. \par \par \uc2  "I find it a little bit difficult to understand when you have a CPI so influenced by housing prices, to try to get inflation down without managing the exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  Ironically, the markets have been positive since last Thursday when Frenkel announced his latest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The Tel Aviv Stock Exchange's benchmark Tel Aviv-100 index has advanced about four percent, which analysts attributed to the strengthening shekel. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also gained about four percent against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "He certainly accomplished part of that," said Goldberg. "But the question is whether he could have done it at a lower price." \par \par \uc2  ((Jerusalem newsroom, +972 2 537-0502, jerusalem.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubh04auc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
