\par\par \b \uc2  Israel/Inflation -3: Recession To Buffer Price Rises \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1998\par 16:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Most economists agree with \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Finance Ministry assessments that the worst of the shekel's decline is now over. \par \par \uc2  "We believe this was a one-time wave and that we'll return to lower inflation rates of 5.0% to 6.0% in 1999 if the depreciation comes to an end," said Eyal Kolka, head of economic research at Sigma P.C.M. Capital Markets Ltd. in Tel Aviv. \par \par \uc2  Victor Shohet, chief economist at Ofek Securities and Investments Ltd. in Tel Aviv, said Israel's 2 1/2-year-old economic slowdown should also prevent recent price markups from translating into a permanent inflation trend. \par \par \uc2  "The recession is actually going to create a situation where the price increases we see are only price adjustments to the new exchange rates," Shohet said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and investors are anxiously awaiting the scheduled announcement Sunday of the October consumer price index and the announcement of the December interest rate, expected to follow eight days later. \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices are expected to have risen 3.5% to 4.0%, and market players foresee a 150- to 200-basis-point rate rise in store for December. \par \par \uc2  "I won't be surprised if we see interest rates rise an additional 400 basis points by the end of the year," Katznelson said. \par \par \uc2  For all that, he said he would prefer if the Bank of Israel were to restore calm to financial markets with other tools "and rely as little as possible on rate rises because under the current slowdown, it comes with a price," he said. \par \par \uc2  Economic growth has slowed to a forecast 1.5% in 1998, from 1.9% in 1997 and 4.5% in 1996. Unemployment has risen as high as 9.5% for the year, from 7.7% in 1997 and 6.7% in 1996. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have sold dollars on the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to calm trade, especially during the shekel's most rapid decline in the first week of October. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't known to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade during the period. \par \par \uc2  Another option would be to abolish the shekel's trading band - a mechanism that allows the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fluctuate 15% on either side of a gradually depreciating midpoint. Media reports have said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seriously considering such a move but was meeting opposition from the Finance Ministry. A Bank of Israel spokesman refused to comment on the reports. \par \par \uc2  -By Michele Gershberg;9722-537-6985;mgershberg@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-11-98 \par \par \uc2  1525GMT \par \par \uc2  1198 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dubc011sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
