\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts see sharp rise in Israeli interest rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 1998\par 17:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Faced with a sudden surge in inflation and continued selling pressure on the shekel, the Bank of Israel will raise interest rates sharply this week, analysts forecast on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "I see a 1.5 percentage point hike, possibly more...The higher the better," said Victor Shohat, chief economist at Ofek Securities & Investments. \par \par \uc2  Other economists said the Bank of Israel could raise rates as little as one point or as much as two points when it announces its November monetary programme on Monday. The base monetary tender rate is currently 9.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The bank said on Sunday it was moving up the announcement to 0630 GMT from the originally scheduled 1430 GMT so that the markets would be able to trade on the basis of the new rate. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Bank of Israel faced the twin task of taming inflation quickly and helping to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The shekel has slid some 14 percent against the dollar over the last 11 weeks, going into a sharp decline at the start of October. Trading remains nervous and volatile, leaving the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cloudy. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hoping a sharp rate hike would obviate the need for direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The Treasury has been absorbing some of the demand for dollars by stepping up the issue of Gilboa dollar-linked convertible bonds. But the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has indicated a reluctance to do so. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Israel wants to send a signal to the market that it will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Amir Haik, chief analyst at \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  Union Bank. \par \par \uc2  He stressed however that would not necessarily be the final word. "If it doesn't work, they'll \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The shekel's losses have quickly upset expectations that annual inflation would fall below four percent this year and could cause further damage in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Haik said that in October alone the consumer price index would rise about 3.5 percent. He said that even if the shekel stablised between 4.1-4.2 to the dollar, 1999 inflation would be 5-6 percent, well above the government's 4.0 percent target. \par \par \uc2  Haik said he was looking for a hike of 1.5-2 points. \par \par \uc2  In addition, economists noted, the rate of money supply growth was too rapid. It expanded 15.9 percent in the 12 months through September. \par \par \uc2  Shohat said the rate hike should prevent a one-off spike in the CPI from becoming long-term inflation. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of higher inflation were already prompting calls from labour unions for wage increases, he said. Meanwhile, retailers were now testing whether consumers would accept sharp price rises for imports. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing the rush into shops, so they (retailers) may get the impression that they can keep raising prices," Shohat said. \par \par \uc2  Shohat said that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled at 4.0-4.1 to the dollar inflation could hold at four percent for 1999 while at 4.3 the CPI would be about six percent. \par \par \uc2  Economists were divided about the preference for raising rates over direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially as a rate hike would mark another about-face for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the last three years, the Bank of Israel has changed the direction of interest rates three times, most recently cutting the base rate a total of 3.9 points in the first eight months of the year. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that greater \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market...would help the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  avoid such a stop-start approach to interest rate setting," said Merrill Lynch in a report. \par \par \uc2  Merrill, which said a one-point rate hike looked to be in the offing, warned that a sharp rate hike would also undermine Israel's tepid economic recovery in 1999. \par \par \uc2  But Shohat was more optimistic, saying the shekel depreciation was good for export competitiveness as long as the rate stabilised at current levels or better. ((Jerusalem newsroom, +972 2 537-0502, jerusalem.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113duap02jrn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
