\par\par \b \uc2  Israel's Frenkel, Neeman Predict Slowing Inflation \b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 1998\par 17:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (Dow Jones)--Israel's top economic policymakers expressed optimism Monday that the rate of inflation will turn lower, despite the shekel's 10% depreciation against the dollar since the end of September. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Yaakov Neeman and Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel made the remarks to Israel radio after speaking to the Knesset Finance Committee on the effects of the depreciation. \par \par \uc2  Both have blamed the shekel's fall on world economic troubles taking a toll on Israel's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. However they said the country's economic fundamentals will allow it to weather the storm. \par \par \uc2  "It's a situation that requires us to keep our fingers on the gauge," Neeman said. "When the market calms down, and I assume that will take some time...we should return to the same level of inflation we saw at the beginning of the year." \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices rose 1.4% in September, following a 1.3% shekel depreciation that month. Analysts forecast an inflation increase of about 2.5% in October due to the sharper depreciation seen so far. The figures have raised 1998 inflation estimates to as high as 8.0%, compared with forecasts of 4.0% to 5.0% that had been held through the end of August. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel said that it would still be possible to reach the government's 1999 inflation target of 4.0%. \par \par \uc2  "There's no doubt that if we implement the correct policy measures we will be able to reach this target," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trading to soften the shekel's fall even during the swiftest period of depreciation in the first week of October. Analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise the 9.5% discount rate by as much as 100 basis points in November to tame the upsurge in inflation. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel is scheduled to announce the November discount rate on Oct. 26. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-10-98 \par \par \uc2  1501GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duaj009pq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
